I had the good fortune and immense joy  of meeting Charles Burnett in person  over a decade ago at the pan African  Film Festival here in Los Angeles I'd  met him years earlier within the frames  of his work of course and felt connected  to this artist who relished sharing the  inner lives of working-class  african-americans on screen in all our  complexity his characters and stories  weren't flat they had round edges and  dimension with Charles my family my  community so often forgotten and  dismissed and ignored felt expansive and  robust and revered so when I met him at  that beautiful black Film Festival with  my very first documentary in 2008 I  wrote in my journal that that night what  how can I look at it and I wrote he was  gentle and generous and full of grace  I met a giant today and he was he  watched my film my not very good film  twice and gave me feedback and kindness  and encouragement and that was like fuel  to me like water for the first time a  major artist to look at my work and say  you have something to say something  valuable to put out in the world in that  moment he made me visible to myself in a  world of visual culture visibility  remains highly political in a much  contested concept that touches upon  issues of identity and representation  ethics and responsibility recognition  and justice Charles Burnett has made the  black community visible and he has been  visible to the black community sir  you've been a giant to us a legend to us  an icon long before today to work to  create to craft your films with no  promise of industry consideration  support or recognition for decades upon  decades is the mark of a true artist  [Applause]  too many times in cinema history books  of which I have many I go to the page to  read about black cinema and so often  it's a paragraph a caption often a still  image with one film that's strong out of  the black cinematic canon to represent  everything that's been done by everyone  and very often it's killer of sheep just  a caption underneath if you're lucky  you'll get a paragraph or two I just  want to let you know mr. Burnett that to  us within the world of that paragraph  you are the universe you're one of the  most significant American directors in  cinema history of any color period your  masterful attention to style your use of  folkloric traditions in your engagement  with the social and political world  around us has centered blackness and  rendered us visible and valuable and  victorious and for that I thank you sir  tonight tonight I am pleased an honor to  salute you Charles Burnett on behalf of  the governors and present you with an  Academy Award
 

